course: everyday
lesson: 338
title: Sweets, change and a friend's toy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Taking something that is not yours is stealing, even when it is small. Harry in Manchester grabbed a handful of sweets from the pick-and-mix because nobody was watching. He felt sick eating them, and Dad found the wrappers in his pocket. They went back together; Harry said sorry to the shopkeeper and paid with his own pocket money.
explain: Some takings are never found out. Harry also took a pound from Mum's purse for a lolly. Nobody noticed, but every time she opened her purse his stomach turned over. On Saturday he told it at confession; the priest gave him absolution (forgiveness in God's name) and a small penance (a short prayer or task to show he is sorry), and he felt the weight lift.
explain: The priest also said: «Now tell your mum, and pay it back.» He did, with jobs around the house. Mum was sad more than angry, and then she hugged him. A pound is small, but trust is big.
explain: A thing you have to hide is a thing you cannot enjoy. Harry's friend's toy car stayed in a drawer because he was scared someone would see it, while his friend searched and cried. Giving it back with a sorry was the only way to feel right again.
fact: Taking from a shop without paying is stealing, even if it is only sweets.
fact: Change from shopping you were sent to do belongs to the person who gave you the money.
fact: Borrowing without asking is taking, and breaking the thing makes it worse.
fact: Lost property still belongs to somebody, who may come looking for it.
fact: Food saved for someone is already theirs.
fact: When nobody owns up, often the whole group pays, so owning up quickly is fairer to everyone.
fact: Putting a theft right means giving back or paying back, and saying sorry to the person.
fact: Confession can lift guilt, but it does not replace giving back what you took.
fact: Things you had to steal are hard to enjoy, because you must hide them.
fact: A parent is often more hurt by being stolen from than by the money itself.
exercise: Why did Harry take sweets from the pick-and-mix? | They looked good and nobody was watching | He thought they were free samples for children to try | The shopkeeper said he could | He was paying for them later
exercise: How was the pick-and-mix taking found out? | Dad found the wrappers in his pocket | The shop's alarm rang loudly at the door as he left | A friend posted it online | The shopkeeper chased him
exercise: What put the sweets right? | Going back, saying sorry and paying with his pocket money | Never going to that shop again, so nobody there would remember him | Throwing the wrappers away | Buying sweets somewhere else
exercise: A boy keeps the change after buying milk for Mum. Whose money is it? | Mum's, because she gave it to him | His, because he did the walking | The shopkeeper's | Nobody's, change doesn't count
exercise: A boy takes his friend's toy car home. Why can't he enjoy it? | He has to hide it so nobody sees | Toy cars stop working at other houses | His friend can track it | It is too small to play with
exercise: A boy borrows his brother's headphones without asking and breaks the wire. What is the fair way to make it right? | Say sorry and replace them with his own money | Put them back carefully and say nothing about the wire | Say they were already broken | Lend his brother a pencil
exercise: Harry took a pound from Mum's purse and nobody noticed. How did he feel? | Sick inside every time she opened her purse | Proud of not being caught by anyone at home | Nothing, because it was only a pound | Happy with his lolly for weeks
exercise: After confession, the priest told Harry to do one more thing. What was it? | Tell his mum and pay the money back | Light a candle in church and then forget all about it | Never buy lollies again | Nothing, it was all forgiven
exercise: A girl takes a shiny pencil case from lost property. Why is that wrong? | Lost things still belong to somebody | Lost property belongs to whoever finds it | Only teachers may take lost things | It is fine if it has no name
exercise: A boy eats the last piece of cake saved for Dad. Why is that taking, not just eating? | The cake was already Dad's | Cake is not allowed after school | The tin was locked | Dad is on a diet
exercise: Stickers go missing from the teacher's drawer, and nobody owns up. What often happens? | The whole class pays until someone owns up | The teacher buys more stickers and forgets about it | Nothing at all happens | Only the quietest child is punished
exercise: Why is it fairer to own up quickly when the whole class is being punished? | Others stop paying for your wrong | You get a reward for owning up | The teacher forgets the whole thing | It is not fairer at all
exercise: A boy spends his sister's birthday money from her piggy bank. What does a piggy bank promise her? | That her money is safe there | That money grows in it if left alone | That anyone may borrow from it | That it belongs to the family
exercise: How did the boy put the piggy-bank theft right? | He paid it all back and said sorry | He put in some buttons so it felt heavy | He told her the bank leaked | He waited until she forgot
exercise: Mum found out about the pound. What hurt her most? | That her son had taken from her and hidden it | That she was short of one pound for the bus fare | That the lolly was unhealthy | That the shop was too far away
test: «Harry got sweets n didn't pay lol, no one saw». Which is right? | It is stealing whether anyone saw or not | It's fine because nobody saw | It's fine for sweets, not for toys | It's only stealing above a pound
test: Mum, counting coins: «this is two quid short. where's the rest??» What should the boy say? | The truth, and give the money back | That the shop gave the wrong change | That he dropped it down a drain on the way | That all the prices in the shop went up today
test: Brother, furious: «you BROKE my headphones, I never said u could have them!» What is the best answer? | «Sorry, I'll buy you new ones with my money» | «They were rubbish anyway and the wire was loose» | «You weren't using them» | «Mum said I could»
test: Priest at confession: «Have you given back what you took?» The boy took a pound from Mum. What does the priest mean? | Confession doesn't replace paying back the pound | He wants the pound for the church | Paying back is not needed after confession | Only money above ten pounds must be given back
test: Friend: «I took a pound from mum's purse last week and she never noticed, so it's fine to do again». What is true? | Not noticing doesn't make it right, and next time she may | It's fine because it's family, and family money is shared by everyone | It's fine if it's only a pound | It's fine once a month
test: Year 3 girl, upset: «that's MY pencil case, I lost it!» What should the boy who took it do? | Give it back, say sorry and maybe add a pen | Say he bought it in a shop | Keep it, because finders keepers | Throw it back into lost property
test: Dad home late: «Where's my cake?» His son ate it. What would make it right? | Say sorry and help bake a new cake | Say the dog jumped up and ate it | Say he never saw it | Say cake is bad for Dad anyway
test: Teacher, cross: «Nobody gets stickers till the person who took them owns up.» What is the fair thing for the taker? | Own up and put them back | Wait for someone else to own up | Blame the class helper for it | Hide them better next time
test: Sister crying: «someone took my birthday money!!» Her brother did. What is the fix? | Own up, pay it all back and say sorry | Put a note in the bank saying «borrowed, will pay back» | Say the piggy bank was empty | Help her look and say nothing
test: Which is TRUE about a stolen toy you have to hide? | You can't enjoy it, and the owner is still missing it | It becomes yours after a week | It is fine if your friend has many toys | Hiding it makes it legal
test: Kid: «it's only small stuff, not real stealing». Which reply is right? | Small stuff is still stealing | Under a pound doesn't count at all | Only shops can ever be stolen from | It isn't stealing from friends
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the appropriation of confectionery from a shop without payment. | It constitutes theft irrespective of the value of the goods | It constitutes theft only where the value exceeds a fixed sum | It is permissible where no person observes it | It is permissible where the goods are consumed at once
exam: Identify the principal obligation which remains to a penitent who has received absolution for the taking of money from a parent. | The restitution of the money and an apology to the parent | The payment of an equivalent sum to the church in place of the parent | No obligation of any description | The avoidance of the parent for some days
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an article deposited in a school's lost property? | It remains the property of its owner | It becomes the property of the finder | It becomes the property of the school forthwith | It belongs to no person whatsoever once lost
exam: Determine the most probable consequence where an item is taken from a classroom and no pupil admits responsibility. | A sanction may be imposed upon the entire class until an admission is made | No consequence of any description ensues | The teacher invariably replaces the item without enquiry | The youngest pupil is held responsible
